#text(font: "Times New Roman")[A group of marketing students are creating a strategy to sell ceiling fans. They determine that the number of sales will vary according to the month of the year. The formula #box(baseline: 50% - 0.25em, image("/imgs/572b4f9f91eb867d74c9", width: 1.666667in, height: 0.531250in)); gives the expected sales, in thousands, according to the month, #emph[t];, where #emph[t]; = 1 represents January, #emph[t]; = 2 is February, and so on.#linebreak();
#strong[a)]; In which month are sales of fans expected to reach 11 600?#linebreak();
#strong[b)]; In which month are sales expected to be the least?#linebreak();
#strong[c)]; Does this formula seem reasonable? Explain.];

solution.text:
#strong[a)]; Solve the equation #box(baseline: 50% - 0.25em, image("/imgs/47ec6efa0150d190d2d8", width: 1.864583in, height: 0.531250in)); for #emph[t];.#linebreak();
#box(baseline: 100% - 0.8em, image("/imgs/31820ef92aa312c87503", width: 2.125000in, height: 2.208333in));#linebreak();
These both represent June. Sales will be 11 600 in June. Since the cosine function is equal to 1 at this point, this is the maximum number of sales.#linebreak();
#strong[b)]; The minimum sales will occur when the cosine function is equal to −1, which occurs when#linebreak();
#box(baseline: 50% - 0.25em, image("/imgs/8290207eb5b3a5222597", width: 0.833333in, height: 1.354167in));#linebreak();
The minimum sales are in December.#linebreak();
#strong[c)]; Yes. You would expect the most sales to occur at the beginning of summer and the least sales in a cold month like December.

